LXNU Architecture Overview

Project: LXNU
Version: 1.6.6.2ɓ
Platform: System-One
Architecture: AMD64
Status: Specification and architecture definition. Implementation and validation are not yet demonstrated.

1. Mission

LXNU is a Linux-based kernel engineering project that applies selected XNU-inspired architectural principles to an independent AMD64 computing platform.

LXNU does not contain Mach source code or Apple code. It does not attempt to reproduce macOS. Linux upstream remains the foundation for kernel functionality, hardware support, and long-term maintainability.

The objective is a coherent kernel platform with clearly defined subsystem ownership, controlled integration, measurable performance, and strong security boundaries.

2. Architectural Foundation

LXNU uses the upstream Linux kernel for its established core capabilities.

Linux upstream provides:

- CPU architecture initialization and hardware enablement.
- Task management and process scheduling.
- Virtual memory and physical memory management.
- Virtual filesystem and filesystem implementations.
- Networking and network protocol infrastructure.
- Device drivers and interrupt handling.
- Synchronization primitives and core kernel facilities.
- Hardware and platform support.

LXNU maintains its own architecture, integration contracts, AMD platform policy, configuration, downstream patches, and validation requirements.

It must not duplicate mature Linux functionality without a documented engineering justification.

3. System Ownership

LXNU

Owns kernel architecture, integration interfaces, downstream patch policy, AMD platform integration, security integration, and kernel validation.

OmniRed

Owns workload classification, resource coordination, CPU/GPU/NPU placement policy, power and thermal coordination, and workload scheduling decisions through approved kernel interfaces.

OmniRed does not replace or independently duplicate the Linux scheduler.

OmniSoft

Owns userspace platform integration, runtime services, application-facing interfaces, and operating-system service coordination.

OmniSilicon

Owns AMD hardware discovery, capability reporting, hardware qualification, and validated platform profiles.

Omni Performance Libraries

Own optimized computational operations, runtime-safe instruction dispatch, correctness validation, and fallback implementations.

An enabled optimization is not proof of a performance improvement. Improvements require reproducible measurements.

4. AMD64 Platform Policy

LXNU targets AMD64 systems using supported AMD processors and graphics hardware.

Production capability must be established through hardware discovery and qualification rather than assumptions based on product names.

The userspace baseline is x86-64-v3. More advanced instruction sets, including AVX-512, require runtime feature detection and operating-system state validation before dispatch.

No binary may execute unsupported instructions on an otherwise supported system.

Scalar or lower-ISA fallbacks must remain available wherever required.

5. Kernel and Language Strategy

Linux upstream remains the kernel foundation.

C remains essential for Linux kernel integration and established kernel interfaces. C++ may be used in suitable supporting components outside the Linux kernel. Stable Rust is strategic for appropriate system services, tooling, security-sensitive components, and selected platform infrastructure.

Rust complements C and C++; it does not replace them wholesale.

Production Rust requires pinned stable toolchains, an explicit MSRV policy, reproducible builds, dependency and license audits, security review, unsafe-code review, and testing.

6. Security Architecture

Security must be integrated into the platform rather than added as a collection of unrelated features.

Candidate mechanisms include:

- Linux Security Modules (LSM).
- Landlock.
- Kernel Lockdown.
- Yama.
- Integrity mechanisms.
- eBPF-based policy where justified and validated.
- Least-privilege userspace service boundaries.

Every security mechanism requires configuration review, compatibility testing, and documented validation.

A configured security feature is not automatically a proven security guarantee.

7. Upstream and Source Management

LXNU must track a precisely pinned upstream Linux revision.

Downstream changes must remain separately identifiable, documented, reviewable, and justified.

The project must preserve upstream copyright notices, SPDX identifiers, and source provenance.

LXNU must not claim ownership of upstream Linux code or impose a blanket license on third-party components.

8. Engineering Requirements

Every subsystem must have:

1. A defined owner.
2. A documented interface.
3. Explicit dependencies.
4. Security considerations.
5. Correctness tests.
6. Reproducible build requirements.
7. Performance benchmarks where performance claims are made.
8. A failure and rollback strategy where applicable.

Architectural complexity must have a measurable purpose.

9. Validation Gates

No component is considered implemented merely because it has been specified.

Validation must include, as applicable:

- Source and configuration audits.
- Reproducible build verification.
- Boot and hardware initialization tests.
- CPU and memory correctness tests.
- Driver and graphics qualification.
- Security testing.
- Workload coordination tests.
- Regression testing.
- Performance measurements against documented baselines.

Unexecuted gates remain NOT RUN.

10. Project Status

LXNU 1.6.6.2ɓ is an architectural specification and engineering project.

No implementation, successful kernel build, boot result, performance improvement, or production release is claimed by this document.

Specified. NOT RUN. No gate measured. No component claimed to ship.

